1	/**
2	 * ============================================================================
3	 * EXPERIMENT: Rung 0a (off-the-shelf) — chunk × retrieve × rank sweep
4	 * ============================================================================
5	 *
6	 * Source plan: reports/hypothesis-test-ladder.md §"Rung 0 · 0a".
7	 * Results go to: reports/metrics/excerpt-window-0a/results-offtheshelf.json
8	 * Companion (bespoke shipped excerptAround baseline): ./sweep.mjs
9	 *
10	 * WHY THIS EXISTS
11	 * ---------------
12	 * Rung 0a's original bespoke sweep tuned a hand-rolled fixed-width excerpt
13	 * (packages/core excerptAround). But passage extraction is a SOLVED, commodity
14	 * problem: chunking, retrieval and ranking all have mature open-source
15	 * implementations. This script replaces the bespoke unit with off-the-shelf
16	 * components and sweeps THEIR parameters to find the best combination.
17	 *
18	 * WHAT IT TESTS (upgraded from the bespoke sweep)
19	 * -----------------------------------------------
20	 * The bespoke sweep tested ONLY chunking: it was handed the already-correct
21	 * event and asked whether one excerpt held the answer. This version tests the
22	 * full unit — CHUNK + RETRIEVE + RANK — by pooling all recovered contexts into
23	 * ONE corpus, so for each query the pipeline must (a) rank chunks from the whole
24	 * corpus and (b) surface one whose text contains the answer.
25	 *
26	 *   corpus     : the N recovered `wide_context` slices, pooled (answer lives in
27	 *                exactly one of them)
28	 *   chunkers   : RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter, TokenTextSplitter (LangChain);
29	 *                semantic percentile-breakpoint (the standard RAG algorithm over
30	 *                MiniLM sentence embeddings); sentence-window / parent-document
31	 *   rankers    : BM25 (wink-bm25-text-search) and dense vector (MiniLM cosine)
32	 *   swept      : chunk size, overlap, semantic breakpoint percentile, window
33	 *                size, and top-k
34	 *   metric     : fraction of queries whose answer_literals ALL appear in the
35	 *                union of the top-k retrieved chunks, vs tokens returned
36	 *
37	 * HONEST CAVEAT (same as the bespoke sweep)
38	 * -----------------------------------------
39	 * The pooled documents are the answer-windowed `wide_context` slices (<= ~2.1KB
40	 * each) — the real s1 event store died with eval/ at 7d459f9. So this measures
41	 * chunker/ranker CHOICE on that corpus; it is not full-event retrieval, and the
42	 * absolute rates are optimistic vs a store of whole multi-KB events. What it
43	 * DOES settle is the relative question the user posed: does an off-the-shelf
44	 * pipeline, swept over its own parameters, beat the bespoke fixed-window unit —
45	 * and which configuration wins.
